diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase sweep of the dc_clos_bgp fabric found no fault. Phase 1 reachability showed 0% loss in both directions between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2). Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) confirmed clean ACL tables, correct host addressing, reachable ARP gateways on both access segments, and no duplicate MAC addresses across all seven devices. Phase B (bgp_snapshot + tc_snapshot) confirmed that all five FRR routers are running watchfrr, zebra, and bgpd; every BGP session in the Clos fabric — both leaf-to-spine pairs and both spine-to-super-spine links — is in Established state with no dropped connections and zero BGP NOTIFICATION messages exchanged. TC snapshot showed only default fq_codel qdiscs with no bandwidth throttling or netem corruption anywhere. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket exhaustion on any device. Detailed BGP neighbor inspection on both leaf routers confirmed that each adjacency has been Established exactly once since the lab started (connections established: 1, dropped: 0), and interface statistics on all leaf-router uplinks showed zero carrier errors, TX/RX drops, or collisions. There is no flap script, no carrier-change history, and no BGP state-machine history consistent with the reported intermittent fault. The team's hypothesis of bouncing leaf–spine BGP adjacencies is unconfirmed by any observable mechanism or trace. Root cause: no anomaly exists in the current lab state; the reported symptom cannot be reproduced or attributed to a device fault. No device needs to be restarted.
=== END ===